city|TH|Normal capacity":["u51"],"c47-reference-capacity|row:Normal capacity|TD|Normal bladder capacity is 300 to 600 mL.":["u52"],"copy|row:Cough stress test|TH|Cough stress test":["u53"],"c47-reference-cough|row:Cough stress test|TD|For the cough stress test, the patient should have a comfortably full bladder at approximately half-capacity of 200 mL.":["u54"],"copy|row:Postvoid residual|TH|Postvoid residual":["u55"],"c47-reference-pvr|row:Postvoid residual|TD|Postvoid residual values greater than 200 mL are abnormal.":["u56"],"copy|row:Intermittent catheterization|TH|Intermittent catheterization":["u57"],"c47-reference-catheter|row:Intermittent catheterization|TD|The goal of intermittent catheterization is to keep residual urine volume generally less than approximately 300 to 400 mL.":["u58"],"copy|CHAPTER 47 / HAZZARD":["u59"],"c47-opening|Work through the time course, symptom pattern, emptying assessment and treatment approach.":["u60"],"c47-opening|Preserve the setting and the measurement whenever a number appears.":["u61"],"c47-community-prevalence|Among community-dwelling older women, the prevalence of any urinary incontinence is approximately 35%.":["u62"],"c47-community-prevalence|Among community-dwelling older men, the prevalence of any urinary incontinence is approximately 22%.":["u63"],"c47-daily-prevalence|The prevalence of daily urinary incontinence in older community-dwelling persons is approximately 12% for women.":["u64"],"c47-daily-prevalence|The prevalence of daily urinary incontinence in older community-dwelling persons is approximately 5% for men.":["u65"],"c47-nursing-home-prevalence|The prevalence of urinary incontinence is higher among nursing home residents approaching 60%.":["u66"],"c47-filling-innervation|Urination is governed by a sacral spinal cord reflex.":["u315"],"c47-filling-innervation|During filling, afferent pathways convey bladder-volume information and motor output adjusts.":["u316"],"c47-filling-innervation|Sympathetic tone closes the bladder neck and inhibits parasympathetic tone, relaxing the bladder dome; somatic innervation maintains pelvic floor and striated periurethral muscle tone.":["u317"],"c47-filling-innervation|Voluntary pelvic floor contraction also inhibits parasympathetic tone.":["u318"],"c47-emptying-innervation|For emptying, sympathetic and somatic tone diminish, while parasympathetic cholinergic impulses contract the bladder.":["u319"],"c47-emptying-innervation|During filling, bladder pressure remains low, at or below 15 cm H2O.":["u337"],"c47-central-control|The brain stem facilitates urination and the cerebral cortex exerts a predominantly inhibitory influence.":["u67"],"c47-filling-pressure|Under normal circumstances, as the bladder fills, bladder pressure remains low at ≤ 15 cm H2O.":["u68"],"c47-first-urge|First urge to void is variable but generally occurs at 150–350 mL.":["u338"],"c47-first-urge|This is the first-urge volume, not normal capacity or postvoid residual.":["u339"],"c47-capacity|Normal bladder capacity is 300 to 600 mL.":["u70"],"c47-voiding-interval|Normal urination is usually initiated every 3 to 4 hours during wakefulness.":["u71"],"c47-detrusor-voiding|During normal voiding, urethral resistance falls immediately before bladder contraction.":["u340"],"c47-detrusor-voiding|The detrusor then contracts and its pressure rises above outlet resistance, allowing urine flow, typically for less than 2 minutes.":["u341"],"c47-leakage-mechanics|Leakage occurs during filling if total bladder pressure exceeds outlet resistance.":["u342"],"c47-leakage-mechanics|Coughing or sneezing can transmit abdominal pressure against a weak outlet; involuntary detrusor contraction is another route to leakage.":["u343"],"c47-involuntary-contractions|Involuntary bladder contractions are found in 40% to 75% of older incontinent patients.":["u72"],"c47-involuntary-contractions|Involuntary bladder contractions are found in 5% to 10% of older continent women.":["u73"],"c47-involuntary-contractions|Involuntary bladder contractions are found in up to one-third of older men with no or minimal urinary symptoms.":["u74"],"c47-age-capacity-residual|Postvoid residual tends to increase with age.":["u344"],"c47-age-capacity-residual|Reduced functional capacity may partly reflect the increased prevalence of involuntary contractions and detrusor overactivity, rather than age alone.":["u345"],"c47-detrusor-anatomy|Detrusor overactivity has been associated with protrusion junctions and ultra-close abutment between detrusor muscle cells on biopsy, and with cortical changes suggesting impaired integration of bladder afferent signals.":["u346"],"c47-contractions-mobility|Involuntary contractions do not invariably cause incontinence.":["u347"],"c47-contractions-mobility|Combined with impaired mobility, however, they likely contribute substantially to incontinence among functionally disabled older people.":["u348"],"c47-female-outlet-ageing|Bladder outlet and urethral resistance pressure decline with ageing in women.":["u349"],"c47-female-outlet-ageing|Childbirth, vaginal or Caesarean, is associated with later incontinence, but the association is weaker after age 65.":["u350"],"c47-vaginal-support|In older populations, poor vaginal support is more closely associated with obstructive urinary symptoms than with leakage or urgency.":["u351"],"c47-female-risk-context|Obesity, muscle deconditioning and hysterectomy predispose to later incontinence.":["u352"],"c47-female-risk-context|The chapter reports greater stress-incontinence prevalence among White women than other racial or ethnic groups; this is a group association, not an individual diagnostic rule.":["u353"],"c47-male-nocturia-ageing|Prostatic enlargement in older men may reduce flow and accompany detrusor instability.":["u354"],"c47-male-nocturia-ageing|Nocturia increases with age, partly because more urine is produced at night.":["u355"],"c47-dhic-entity|Detrusor hyperactivity with impaired contractility combines involuntary detrusor activity with weak emptying.":["u356"],"c47-dhic-entity|An older person can therefore be wet and retaining urine at the same time; biopsy may show widespread detrusor muscle degeneration.":["u357"],"c47-dhic-measurement-scope|The less-than-one-third finding describes emptying during involuntary contractions in a tested subgroup.":["u358"],"c47-dhic-measurement-scope|It is not a universal residual-volume cutoff for every patient with urgency.":["u359"],"c47-time-course|Acute incontinence refers to those situations where the incontinence is of sudden onset, usually linked to an acute illness or an iatrogenic problem, and subsides once the illness or problem has been resolved.":["u75"],"c47-time-course|Persistent incontinence refers to incontinence that is not precipitated by an acute illness and endures over time.":["u76"],"c47-stress-definition|The symptoms of stress incontinence are specific and involve leakage coincident with increases in intra-abdominal pressure caused by coughing, sneezing, laughing, or exercising.":["u77"],"c47-stress-men|Stress incontinence is unusual in men, and it mainly occurs following transurethral interventions for benign prostatic conditions or after surgical or radiation therapy for lower urinary tract malignancy.":["u78"],"c47-urgency-definition|Urgency incontinence, when it occurs along with urinary urgency, daytime urinary frequency, and nocturia, has been called wet overactive bladder.":["u79"],"c47-urgency-without-contractions|Urgency incontinence is often, but not always, associated with involuntary detrusor contractions.":["u360"],"c47-urgency-without-contractions|Poor bladder compliance without such contractions is another mechanism, as can occur with interstitial cystitis or after irradiation.":["u361"],"c47-retention-definitions|Acute retention of urine is defined as a painful, palpable or percussable bladder, when the patient is unable to pass any urine.":["u80"],"c47-retention-definitions|Chronic retention of urine is defined as a non-painful bladder, which remains palpable or percussable after the patient has passed urine.":["u81"],"c47-detrusor-sphincter-dyssynergia|Suprasacral spinal cord disease, such as multiple sclerosis, can cause detrusor–sphincter dyssynergia and retention.":["u362"],"c47-detrusor-sphincter-dyssynergia|The chapter describes management along the lines of overflow incontinence, with sphincterotomy occasionally needed.":["u363"],"c47-functional-definition|Functional incontinence results when an older person is unable to reach a toilet on time due to impaired cognitive function and/or mobility.":["u82"],"c47-oral-estrogen-risk|Oral estrogen therapy has been identified as a risk factor for subsequent development of urinary incontinence in women.":["u83"],"c47-medication-ocr-limit|The rendered header identifies the medication-effects table as TABLE 47-4.":["u203"],"c47-medication-ocr-limit|TABLE 47-5 is the separate classification of persistent incontinence.":["u204"],"c47-retention-examination|In acute urinary retention, the distended bladder is a firm, midline mass that emanates from the pelvis and is dull to percussion.":["u86"],"c47-distention-volume|Gross bladder distention of ≥ 500 mL should be easily detected on physical examination.":["u87"],"c47-cough-position|The sensitivity of the cough stress test is highest when the patient is standing.":["u88"],"c47-cough-volume|For the cough stress test, the patient should have a comfortably full bladder at approximately half-capacity of 200 mL.":["u89"],"c47-cough-delay|During a cough stress test, delayed leakage after 3 seconds or the initiation of voiding generally indicates a cough-induced bladder contraction.":["u90"],"c47-pvr-low|Postvoid residual values of less than 100 mL in the absence of straining to void generally reflect adequate bladder emptying in geriatric patients.":["u91"],"c47-pvr-high|Postvoid residual values greater than 200 mL are abnormal.":["u92"],"c47-assessment-ocr-guide|These are selected-patient investigations from TABLE 47-6, not the diagnostic flowchart in FIGURE 47-2.":["u205"],"c47-assessment-ocr-guide|They are not all routine tests for every patient.":["u206"],"c47-refer-hematuria-reason|The Urinalysis referral criterion is greater than or equal to 3 red blood cells per high-power field on microscopic examination in the absence of infection.":["u369"],"c47-refer-hematuria-reason|It prompts exclusion of a pathological condition in the upper urinary tract, bladder or urethra and is separate from the postvoid-residual referral section.":["u370"],"c47-assessment-pathway-pending|The p1558 material continues TABLE 47-6 from its title page at p1557; the postvoid-residual controversy footnote, “See also Table 47-7” and Kane/Ouslander credit belong to this table, not FIGURE 47-2.":["u207"],"c47-assessment-pvr-footnote|The recommendation for a postvoid residual determination in all incontinent elderly patients is controversial (see text).":["u176"],"c47-chronic-bacteriuria|Urine loss among older patients with chronic incontinence with otherwise asymptomatic bacteriuria does not appear to improve with bacteriuria treatment.":["u96"],"c47-chronic-bacteriuria|In nursing home populations, there is no benefit to treating bacteriuria in patients with chronic, stable incontinence.":["u97"],"c47-first-line|Multicomponent interventions, including lifestyle and behavioral therapies, are effective first-line treatments for incontinence in the older adult.":["u98"],"c47-weight-loss|In women there is evidence that modest weight loss of approximately 5% to 10% of body weight leads to reduction in urinary incontinence episodes.":["u99"],"c47-pelvic-floor|Pelvic floor muscle exercises are effective in men and women for the treatment of urge, stress, mixed stress-urge incontinence, and incontinence following prostatectomy.":["u100"],"c47-pelvic-floor-duration|Pelvic floor muscle exercises should be practiced many times throughout the day, building up from 3 seconds to 10 seconds in duration.":["u101"],"c47-scheduled-toileting|Scheduled toileting involves assisting the patient to the toilet at regular intervals, usually every 2 to 3 hours during the day regardless of the presence or absence of the patient's expressed desire to void.":["u102"],"c47-habit-training|Habit training involves a schedule of toileting that is individually modified according to the patient's pattern of continent voids and incontinence episodes.":["u103"],"c47-prompted-voiding|Prompted voiding involves asking the patient to attempt to void up to three times every 2 hours during the day.":["u104"],"c47-prompted-response|Between 25% and 40% of nursing home residents respond very well to daytime prompted voiding.":["u105"],"c47-drug-classes|Drugs with bladder smooth-muscle relaxant properties used for urgency incontinence are available in two different classes: antimuscarinic and beta-3-agonist.":["u106"],"c47-drug-effect|Most studies suggest a reduction of 60% to 70% in the frequency of incontinence episodes with drug therapy in selected older adults.":["u107"],"c47-antimuscarinic-dementia|Multiple epidemiologic studies suggest bladder antimuscarinic drug therapy is associated with a 50% relative increase in the odds of developing dementia.":["u108"],"c47-beta3-caution|TABLE 47-12 lists hypertension and tachycardia for mirabegron.":["u208"],"c47-beta3-caution|Its vibegron entry instead names headache and advises avoiding use in severe renal or hepatic impairment.":["u209"],"c47-beta3-caution|These are drug-specific entries, not a class-wide hypertension exclusion.":["u210"],"c47-older-men|For older men with overactive bladder symptoms, alpha adrenergic antagonists may be a better choice for first-line drug therapy.":["u110"],"c47-estrogen-worsening|Either opposed or unopposed oral conjugated estrogen therapy has been shown to increase the risk of developing or worsening incontinence.":["u111"],"c47-retention-drug-limit|Drug treatment in the setting of chronic urinary retention or urinary incontinence with high PVR, with either a cholinergic agonist or an alpha-adrenergic antagonist, is usually not efficacious.":["u371"],"c47-drug-doses-pending|Darifenacin and fesoterodine doses remain withheld.":["u211"],"c47-drug-doses-pending|The confirmed rows below reproduce the chapter’s printed regimens and qualifications; they are not a complete prescribing monograph.":["u212"],"c47-mirabegron-printed-cap|As printed in TABLE 47-12: mirabegron maximum 25 mg/day with CrCl 15–20 and with reduced hepatic function.":["u213"],"c47-mirabegron-printed-cap|The adverse-effects entry names hypertension and tachycardia.":["u214"],"c47-tibial-stimulation|Percutaneous tibial nerve stimulation involves weekly 30-minute stimulation sessions in an outpatient clinical setting for up to 12 weeks.":["u115"],"c47-botulinum-duration|Early trial results suggest that botulinum toxin A therapy lasts 6 months and is equally effective in older and younger age groups.":["u116"],"c47-intermittent-target|The goal of intermittent catheterization is to keep residual urine volume generally less than approximately 300 to 400 mL.":["u117"],"c47-intermittent-cost-premise|Using sterile catheter trays for repeated intermittent catheterisation would be very expensive in a typical nursing home programme.":["u364"],"c47-intermittent-clinical-premises|Other practical barriers compound the expense: older nursing-home residents, particularly men, can be difficult to catheterise; anatomic abnormalities increase infection risk with repeated straight catheterisation; and institutional settings harbour organisms relatively resistant to commonly used antimicrobials.":["u365"],"c47-intermittent-feasibility-conclusion|Together, these cost, anatomical and infection-control difficulties can make an intermittent catheterisation programme extremely difficult to implement in a typical nursing home.":["u366"],"c47-intermittent-feasibility-conclusion|This feasibility argument does not itself establish an indication for a chronic indwelling catheter.":["u367"],"c47-catheter-changing|The limited evidence available to date does not suggest that routine changing of indwelling bladder catheters is warranted.":["u118"],"c47-catheter-risk-context|Chronic indwelling catheterization carries higher risk than other forms of management and should be used sparingly; suprapubic catheterization may be preferred to urethral catheterization for chronic use.":["u215"],"c47-catheter-long-term-risks|Over months to years, chronic catheterization increases chronic bacteriuria, bladder stones, periurethral abscesses, urethral injury including penile erosion, and bladder cancer.":["u216"],"c47-catheter-long-term-risks|Limited evidence does not support routine scheduled catheter changes, although the practice is common.":["u217"],"c47-catheter-indications-pending|Urinary retention causing persistent overflow incontinence, symptomatic infections or renal dysfunction, which cannot be corrected surgically or medically and cannot practically be managed with intermittent catheterization.":["u218"],"c47-fecal-cooccurrence|Thirty to fifty percent of older patients in institutional settings with frequent urinary incontinence also have episodes of fecal incontinence.":["u121"],"c47-constipation-definition|Constipation technically indicates fewer than three bowel movements per week.":["u122"],"c47-reference-guide|Keep the procedure and the time of measurement in the same sentence as the volume.":["u123"],"c47-reference-guide|These entries repeat the source findings for comparison.":["u124"]}}
